
"smart_sources:Lore for Flora's Regency Era (UPDATED)/meta/Contextual Rules 2.md": {"path":"Lore for Flora's Regency Era (UPDATED)/meta/Contextual Rules 2.md","last_embed":{"hash":null},"embeddings":{},"last_read":{"hash":"lvdkdm","at":1777331817895},"class_name":"SmartSource","last_import":{"mtime":1776992722668,"size":2648,"at":1777331817922,"hash":"lvdkdm"},"blocks":{"#---frontmatter---":[1,14],"#Contextual Rules 2":[16,31],"#Contextual Rules 2#{1}":[18,31]},"outlinks":[],"metadata":{"type":"lore","status":"active","priority":120,"tags":["#lorebook","#lorebook-always"],"position":"after","depth":4,"scanDepth":1,"summary":"**What this is:** Codified legal and social structures governing Flora's matriarchal society. Defines political power, inheritance, and marital norms.\n\n**When to select:** Any mention of Flora's government, nobility, inheritance, marriage, divorce, or social hierarchy. Especially relevant for characters interacting with noble houses, succession disputes, or political alliances.\n\n**Key relationships:**\n- **Matriarchs:** Female heads of noble houses; hold highest political power and govern counties.\n- **Bouquet of the Roses:** National assembly of Matriarchs that creates laws.\n- **Crown Princess:** Ceremonial monarch with limited direct power; rules through influence and alliances.\n- **Heirs:** Only daughters inherit titles, lands, and family names. Sons cannot inherit primary estate or title.\n- **Marriage:** Political alliance tool; shared husband creates alliance between houses, not legal marriage between wives. Divorce rare and ruinous."},"task_lines":[],"tasks":{},"codeblock_ranges":[]},"smart_blocks:Lore for Flora's Regency Era (UPDATED)/meta/Contextual Rules 2.md#---frontmatter---": {"path":null,"last_embed":{"hash":null},"embeddings":{},"text":null,"length":0,"last_read":{"hash":"u0wray","at":1777331817895},"key":"Lore for Flora's Regency Era (UPDATED)/meta/Contextual Rules 2.md#---frontmatter---","lines":[1,14],"size":1123,"outlinks":[],"class_name":"SmartBlock"},
